[PROPOSAL — NOT APPLIED] recommend() ranks on filmography; give it the map's own policy
=======================================================================================

A design agent owns atlas/app/template.html, so this is the measured proposal
rather than an edit. It is a real `git apply` patch and it was verified to apply
cleanly to the working tree at the time of writing, applied, measured, and
reverted byte-identically.

    git apply atlas/proposals/recommender-rule1.patch
    node atlas/pipeline/measure-recommender.js            # must go FAIL -> PASS
    node atlas/pipeline/measure-recommender.js --selftest

and, in the same commit, add it to the release command — it is left OUT of
`npm test` deliberately, because it FAILS at HEAD and wiring a failing gate into
the build would break it for whoever is holding the template:

    "test:atlas-recommender": "node atlas/pipeline/measure-recommender.js --selftest && node atlas/pipeline/measure-recommender.js"

WHAT IS WRONG
-------------
`recommend()` is the only ranked surface in the app that does not use
SIGNAL_WEIGHT. It scores `strength * (authored ? 1.2 : 1)` and takes the top
twelve. `connections()`, thirty lines above it in the same file, already ships
three devices against exactly this failure, each argued for at length in the
source: sigWeight, a x0.55 same-hand penalty ("same hand is the least surprising
link"), and a per-director cap with an uncapped second pass.

Raw strength is very nearly a test for "is this a production credit". Measured
at HEAD: same-director edges carry median strength 0.699 against 0.558 for
everything else, and 3,106 of the corpus's 5,605 crew edges join two films by
the same director. So a scorer that ranks on strength alone ranks on
filmography, and the shelf fills with the director you already picked.

MEASURED — 300 seeded three-film profiles, 3,600 suggestions at the shipped
limit of 12, by pipeline/measure-recommender.js, which parses recommend() and
connections() out of template.html rather than copying them:

                                        HEAD      patched     map view, same K
    share a director with a loved film  62.6%      20.9%        17.0%
    type = hand                         67.4%      44.3%
    industrial signal (crew + cast)     67.4%      43.9%        52.8%
    trivia signal (measure-claims' set)  7.2%       1.4%        46.5%
    authored claim carries the "why"    15.3%      21.1%
    keyword claim carries the "why"      6.7%      18.8%
    most films by one director on a shelf 3.97      1.77         (of 12)
    shelves that came back short             0         0
    distinct films ever suggested        1,611      1,493        (of 2,204)

    gate: sameDirector vs the map view   3.69x      1.23x        ceiling 2.00x
    gate: worst shelf, one director       3.97       1.77        ceiling 3.00

(The workflow brief quotes 58.8% and 70.4% from an earlier harness with a
different profile seed. Same phenomenon, same magnitude; the seed is fixed at
0x5eed here so the number is reproducible.)

THE COST, WHICH IS REAL
-----------------------
Distinct films ever suggested falls 1,611 -> 1,493 of 2,204. Demoting crew and
cast concentrates the shelf on the 1,839 films that carry TMDB keywords, so the
recommender becomes more specific and slightly narrower in the same move. If
that trade is judged wrong the lever is SIGNAL_WEIGHT.cast (0.24), not the
director cap — the cap costs no coverage on its own.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX
----------------------
The shelf still rests on crew for 42.5% of its "why" lines, because 55.4% of the
corpus's crew edges are same-director edges and re-ranking cannot remove an edge
that is there. That is a corpus problem, in pipeline/associate.js, and it is
priced separately. This patch stops the recommender AMPLIFYING it; it does not
stop the graph containing it.

